<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Labrador</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Labrador is the mainland part of the Canadian province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> and Labrador, a sparsely populated region of tundra and forest on the Atlantic coast. Long inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innu">Innu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a>, its interior was disputed between Newfoundland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judicial-committee-of-the-privy-council">Judicial Committee of the Privy Council</a> decided in Newfoundland's favour in 1927. Its iron ore and the Churchill Falls hydroelectric plant have made it economically important.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/labrador.jpg" alt="Labrador" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph B · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Labrador-Region.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Labrador is the mainland part of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> and Labrador. It makes up 71 per cent of the province's area and is separated from the island of Newfoundland by the Strait of Belle Isle. Its shape is roughly triangular, and it covers the easternmost section of the Canadian Shield, a region of thin soil and abundant mineral resources. Its western border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> follows the drainage divide of the Labrador Peninsula, so that land draining into the Atlantic belongs to Labrador and land draining into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-bay">Hudson Bay</a> to Quebec. The Torngat Mountains in the north hold Mount Caubvick, the highest point in the province. Its indigenous peoples include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a> of Nunatsiavut and NunatuKavut and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innu">Innu</a> of Nitassinan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Norsemen were probably the first Europeans to sight the coast, around 1000 CE, and called the area Markland. The name Labrador comes from the Portuguese explorer João Fernandes Lavrador. In 1542 Basque mariners came ashore on the Strait of Belle Isle and set up a whaling station at what is now Red Bay, where the whaling ship San Juan sank in 1565 and was raised in 1978. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-church">Moravian Brethren</a> came to the coast in 1760 to minister to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a> and founded Nain, Okak, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebron">Hebron</a>, Hopedale and Makkovik.</p><p>Labrador was part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1763 transferred it to the British, who ruled it as part of the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> and from 1791 as part of Lower Canada. In 1809 the British imperial government detached it from Lower Canada and gave it to the Newfoundland Colony. Because the inland boundary had never been precisely stated, the dispute with Canada was referred to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judicial-committee-of-the-privy-council">Judicial Committee of the Privy Council</a>, whose decision of 1927 set the boundary mostly along the coastal watershed. Newfoundland joined Canada in 1949.</p><p>In October 1943 a German U-boat crew installed an automated weather station near Cape Chidley, the only known armed German military operation on the North American mainland during the war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> built a major air force base at Goose Bay during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Iron ore deposits found in western Labrador and adjacent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> in the first half of the 20th century gave rise to the towns of Labrador City and Wabush. In the 1960s the Churchill River was diverted at Churchill Falls, flooding the area now called the Smallwood Reservoir and supplying a hydroelectric station. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a> self-government region of Nunatsiavut was created in 2005. Red Bay is regarded as one of the best-preserved examples of a 16th-century Basque whaling station.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Labrador” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first Europeans Inuit met were the Norse of Greenland; later, Basque whalers worked the <b>Labrador</b> coast, and Inuit raided their stations in winter for iron.</blockquote><a href="/culture/inuit">Inuit · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>…Maryland&quot; and &quot;Florida, My Florida&quot;, of &quot;Michigan, My Michigan&quot; and &quot;The Song of Iowa&quot;, of the &quot;Ode to <b>Labrador</b>&quot;, of college songs in the United States and China, of the Boy Scouts' &quot;Scout Vespers&quot;, of a Swedish schoolchildren's song about the bus driver, and of &quot;Democrats, Good Democrats&quot;,…</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-tannenbaum">O Tannenbaum</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1927 the Privy Council awarded it the interior of <b>Labrador</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-newfoundland">Dominion of Newfoundland</a></li></ul></section>
